1. Detailed UI/UX foundation audit
Good foundation already exists

Central tokens, reusable CSS, keyboard focus, responsive navigation and reduced-motion support.



Visual consistency is incomplete

Some homepage styles still have older colours, menu focus rings use gold, and skeleton animations have different timings.



Responsive experience needs closer review

The homepage search, header search and fixed quick-action dock overlap in purpose. Actual rendered desktop/tablet/mobile behaviour still needs visual review.



The important gaps, in priority order
Priority	Finding	What should improve
P1	Old homepage colours	Move remaining old berry and surface literals to the Rosewood & Almond tokens.
P1	Gold focus rings	Use the same Rosewood focus treatment across menus, fields and buttons.
P1	Duplicate search/navigation	Give the homepage one clear primary search and simplify redundant quick actions.
P1	Incomplete verifier coverage	Extend the existing design-system checks to detect these mismatches rather than allowing them to pass unnoticed.
P2	Animation timing	Use the shared 1400 ms skeleton token in all skeleton modules; reconcile the legacy 180 ms transition with the 140/220/420 ms motion system.
P2	Small menu typography	Increase the 8–10 px menu labels and descriptions to more comfortable reading sizes.
P2	Repeated global styles	Consolidate overlapping scroll, body and selection declarations while preserving existing accessibility contracts.

2. Specific files where the problems exist
A. Homepage palette is not fully synchronized

The updated palette tokens are saved, but several homepage styles still contain hard-coded earlier colours:
- HomeSearch.module.css — previous berry shades and old blush surface values.
- HomeQuickDiscovery.module.css — old hard-coded surface and accent colours.
- HomeHero.module.css — dark charcoal overlays remain. Overlays are useful for property-photo readability, but the colour treatment should better match the Rosewood brand without changing the existing image.
- HomeFooter.module.css — #0D0D0D is used as the large footer background. This is the clearest mismatch with your chosen palette; a Rosewood-led footer would be more cohesive.
Recommended rule: Rosewood is the dominant brand colour, while Almond Cream remains the main page background. Ballet Blush should be a deliberate soft accent, and Espresso Cocoa should be reserved for small lines and dots. Making every background Rosewood would make the site too heavy.

B. Focus rings are still inconsistent
- GlobalMenus.module.css contains three hard-coded champagne/gold outlines.
- MegaMenu.module.css contains hard-coded gold focus shadows/outlines.
- SystemComponents.module.css uses a separate accent-mix focus style.
These should all use the shared --homio-focus-ring and --homio-focus-shadow tokens where appropriate.

C. The current motion rules need a single owner
- foundation.css now uses --motion-skeleton-duration: 1400ms.
- SharedSystem.module.css uses 1.4s.
- SystemComponents.module.css still uses 1.35s.
The first two agree, the third does not. The spinner's 850 ms animation can remain separate because it serves a different purpose. The fix is to unify equivalent animations, not force every animation to have the same duration.

D. Navigation and search need a UX decision

The current Header.tsx always shows the global search, while HomeHero.tsx includes its own Pune-first search. The fixed GlobalActionLayer.tsx also repeats Home, Search and Projects actions.

That can make the page feel crowded, especially on mobile. My recommendation is to give the homepage hero the primary search role, use a more compact header search treatment where needed, and make the fixed quick-action dock intentional rather than redundant. The existing route and interaction behaviour must be preserved when this is implemented.
3. Is the chosen palette readable?
Yes—the four colours themselves are a good choice. I calculated these approximate contrast ratios from the current token values:
Combination	Contrast
Rosewood on Almond Cream	8.32:1
Rosewood on Ballet Blush	7.23:1
Muted Rosewood text on Almond Cream	5.04:1

These combinations clear the WCAG 2.2 AA minimum of 4.5:1 for normal text. 
W3C


One exception to handle deliberately is the disabled-text token: its contrast is about 2.12:1 against Almond. That is acceptable only for genuinely disabled controls—not for ordinary descriptions, labels or important information. 
W3C

4. Why did CI pass if there are still gaps?
The latest Frontend CI run #678 is successful. It ran the existing verification pipeline, TypeScript, lint and production build.

The problem is that passing tests does not necessarily mean every visual detail is consistent. For example, the current verify-design-system.mjs checks canonical tokens and some shared contracts, but does not reject all old hard-coded accents or enforce one skeleton timing across every module.

The verifier should be strengthened; creating a second verifier or another frontend workflow would be the wrong approach.
5. What should happen next?
Continue with APP-001-T01 only. Do not advance to APP-001-T02 yet.

The next implementation batch should synchronize these existing files—without creating duplicates:

tokens.css, base.css, globals.css, foundation.css, motion.css, GlobalMenus.module.css, MegaMenu.module.css, SharedSystem.module.css, SystemComponents.module.css, HomeSearch.module.css, HomeQuickDiscovery.module.css, HomeHero.module.css, HomeFooter.module.css, verify-design-system.mjs and verify-foundation.mjs.

The key design decision is to preserve the four-colour palette, migrate legacy hard-coded brand shades, remove the redundant UI elements where appropriate, and strengthen the existing regression checks. The two verifier files must be updated in the same batch as the implementation so their contracts remain accurate.

After those changes are saved, rerun the existing Frontend CI and inspect the rendered experience at desktop, tablet and mobile widths. A successful build is necessary, but it is not a substitute for that visual review.

Final verdict: the foundation has the right building blocks, but it is not yet fully synchronized or ready to call world-class. Finishing these corrections as part of APP-001-T01 is the right next step. I have not changed any files during this audit, and I have not manually edited state.json.
















